1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,675
Chapter 50: Critical Care and Trauma.

3
00:00:05,675 --> 00:00:23,262
Critical Care Acute Pulmonary Edema Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome Sepsis And Septic Shock Trauma Thermal Injury Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation References Treatment of the critically ill pregnant woman has improved over the past three decades with advances in intensive care capabilities.

4
00:00:23,262 --> 00:00:33,487
To achieve optimal outcomes, severe medical, surgical, and obstetrical disorders complicating pregnancy are frequently managed by a multidisciplinary team.

5
00:00:33,487 --> 00:00:41,662
Importantly, obstetricians and these other health-care team members must have a working knowledge of the unique considerations for pregnancy .

6
00:00:41,662 --> 00:00:53,350
Some discussed in Chapter 49 include pregnancy-induced physiological changes, alterations in normal laboratory values, and consideration for the second patient—the fetus.

7
00:00:53,350 --> 00:00:59,312
Because critically ill women are usually young and healthy, their prognosis is generally good .

8
00:00:59,312 --> 00:01:01,100
Critical Care.

9
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:06,850
Despite noted gains, evidence suggests that severe morbidity rates are increasing .

10
00:01:06,850 --> 00:01:13,512
In the United States, 1 to 3 percent of pregnant women require critical care services each year.

11
00:01:13,512 --> 00:01:18,425
The risk of death during such admissions ranges from 2 to 11 percent .

12
00:01:18,425 --> 00:01:22,537
Black women and Medicaid recipients are disparately affected .

13
00:01:22,537 --> 00:01:36,325
Those with pregnancy-associated complications—especially hemorrhage, sepsis, and hypertension—have the greatest need for intensive care (Chantry, 2015; Guntupalli, 2015a,b).

14
00:01:36,325 --> 00:01:54,237
That said, many antepartum admissions are for nonobstetrical reasons, and these include diabetes, pneumonia or asthma, heart disease, chronic hypertension, pyelonephritis, and thyrotoxicosis (Guntupalli, 2015b; Zeeman, 2006).

15
00:01:54,237 --> 00:02:01,137
Cardiac disease is the leading non-obstetrical indication for intensive care unit (ICU) admission .

16
00:02:01,137 --> 00:02:10,137
In instances of life-threatening hemorrhage, surgical procedures may be necessary, and close proximity to an operating room is paramount.

17
00:02:10,137 --> 00:02:19,150
For women who are undelivered, fetal well-being also is better served by this close proximity, especially because many are delivered preterm .

18
00:02:19,150 --> 00:02:22,350
# Organization of Critical Care.

19
00:02:22,350 --> 00:02:29,575
The concept and development of critical care for all aspects of medicine and surgery began in the 1960s.

20
00:02:29,575 --> 00:02:43,600
As a part of this effort, the National Institutes of Health held a Consensus Conference (1983) and the Society of Critical Care Medicine (1988, 1999) established guidelines for ICUs.

21
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:51,275
Especially pertinent to obstetrics, these costly units prompted the evolution of a step-down intermediate care unit.

22
00:02:51,275 --> 00:03:00,350
These latter units were designed for patients who did not require intensive care but who needed a higher level of treatment than that provided on a general ward.

23
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:09,662
The American College of Critical Care Medicine and the Society of Critical Care Medicine (1998) have published guidelines for these units .

24
00:03:09,662 --> 00:03:11,987
Table 50-1.

25
00:03:11,987 --> 00:03:18,450
Guidelines for Conditions That Could Qualify for Intermediate Care # Obstetrical Critical Care.

26
00:03:18,450 --> 00:03:27,887
Although critical care for obstetrical patients has generally evolved along the path described in the last section, no detailed guidelines direct this care.

27
00:03:27,887 --> 00:03:34,862
Most hospitals employ a blend of these concepts, and in general, units can be divided into three types.

28
00:03:34,862 --> 00:03:41,712
First, medical and surgical ICUs in most hospitals care for severely ill obstetrical patients.

29
00:03:41,712 --> 00:03:46,800
These are staffed by specialists often certified in critical care medicine.

30
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:55,612
Admissions or transfers to these units are situation-specific and based on the acuity of care needed and on the ability of the facility to provide it.

31
00:03:55,612 --> 00:04:06,662
For example, pregnant women who require ventilatory support, invasive monitoring, or pharmacological support of circulation are typically transferred to an ICU .

32
00:04:06,662 --> 00:04:17,862
In an earlier review of more than 25 tertiary-care referral institutions, approximately 0.5 percent of obstetrical patients were transferred to these ICU types .

33
00:04:17,862 --> 00:04:22,087
A second option is the obstetrical intermediate care unit.

34
00:04:22,087 --> 00:04:28,250
Sometimes referred to as a high-dependency care unit, this type is found at Parkland Hospital.

35
00:04:28,250 --> 00:04:35,075
Located within the labor and delivery unit, it has designated rooms staffed by experienced personnel.

36
00:04:35,075 --> 00:04:38,250
This two-tiered system incorporates the

37
00:04:38,404 --> 00:04:41,991
guidelines for intermediate and intensive care.

38
00:04:41,941 --> 00:04:50,579
Care is provided by maternalfetal medicine specialists, obstetricians, and nurses with experience in critical care obstetrics.

39
00:04:50,579 --> 00:05:05,741
As needed, this team is expanded to include other obstetricians and anesthesiologists, hospitalists, gynecological oncologists, pulmonologists, cardiologists, surgeons, and other medical and surgical subspecialists .

40
00:05:05,741 --> 00:05:13,179
Many tertiary-care centers have developed similar intermediate care units and use selected triage to ICUs.

41
00:05:13,179 --> 00:05:18,266
Last, a few services have a full-care ICU as described first.

42
00:05:18,266 --> 00:05:28,379
However, it is distinctly operated by obstetrical and anesthesia personnel near the labor and delivery unit (Zeeman, 2003, 2006).

43
00:05:28,379 --> 00:05:40,454
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) has summarized critical obstetrical care implementation depending on hospital size and technical facilities.

44
00:05:40,454 --> 00:05:48,616
For intrahospital transfers, the federal Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act (EMTALA) guidelines must be followed.

45
00:05:48,616 --> 00:06:08,366
Moreover, according to the American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017), the minimal monitoring required for a critically ill patient during transport includes continuous pulse oximetry, electrocardiography, and regular assessment of vital signs.

46
00:06:08,366 --> 00:06:17,166
Patients must have secure venous access, and those who are mechanically ventilated must have endotracheal tube position confirmed and secured.

47
00:06:17,166 --> 00:06:24,466
Left uterine displacement and supplemental oxygen is applied routinely during transport of antepartum patients.

48
00:06:24,466 --> 00:06:29,879
Continuous fetal heart rate or tocodynamic monitoring is individualized.

49
00:06:29,879 --> 00:06:32,866
# Severe Maternal Morbidity.

50
00:06:32,866 --> 00:06:37,491
Childbirth can result in significant morbidity for the pregnant woman.

51
00:06:37,491 --> 00:06:47,516
To help address this, severe maternal morbidity (SMM) was developed as a population-based monitoring standard for the most common of these complications .

52
00:06:47,516 --> 00:07:00,029
An overall SMM rate is derived from 18 SMM indicators, which are abstracted from International Classification of Diseases-10 (ICD-10) codes in administrative databases.

53
00:07:00,029 --> 00:07:04,929
SMM rates for an individual indicator also may be informative.

54
00:07:04,929 --> 00:07:08,691
Some indicators are found in Table 50-1.

55
00:07:08,691 --> 00:07:21,216
and the full list is available at www.cdc.gov/reproductivehealth/maternalinfanthealth/smm/severemorbidity-ICD.htm.

56
00:07:21,216 --> 00:07:27,854
Several of these are included in this chapter, whereas others are addressed in specific chapters on their topic.

57
00:07:27,854 --> 00:07:34,141
The goal of SMM monitoring is to identify opportunities for intervention and prevention.

58
00:07:34,141 --> 00:07:38,329
Despite its promise, limitations should be recognized.

59
00:07:38,329 --> 00:07:44,466
First, the rate does not factor underlying patient comorbidities and obstetrical risks.

60
00:07:44,466 --> 00:07:54,054
Thus, a tertiary-care center that serves as a referral center for women with placenta accreta spectrum will understandably have higher blood transfusion rates.

61
00:07:54,054 --> 00:08:00,379
Second, the ICD-10 indicators for existing SMM events are not comprehensive.

62
00:08:00,379 --> 00:08:09,929
For example, only codes for total abdominal hysterectomy are currently included in the SMM rate, whereas supracervical hysterectomy is not.

63
00:08:09,929 --> 00:08:17,991
With these limitations in mind, caution should be exercised when using SMM as a quality metric to compare hospitals .

64
00:08:17,991 --> 00:08:21,579
# Hemodynamic Changes in Pregnancy.

65
00:08:21,579 --> 00:08:35,129
Data obtained during pregnancy with pulmonary artery catheterization (PAC) have contributed immensely to the understanding of normal pregnancy hemodynamics and pathophysiology of common obstetrical conditions.

66
00:08:35,129 --> 00:08:45,191
Clark and coworkers (1989) used PAC to obtain cardiovascular measurements in healthy pregnant women and again in these same women when nonpregnant .

67
00:08:45,191 --> 00:08:59,904
Other evaluated conditions include preeclampsiaeclampsia, acute respiratory distress syndrome, and amnionic-fluid embolism (Clark, 1997; Cunningham, 1987; Hankins, 1985).

68
00:08:59,904 --> 00:09:14,404
Because of these studies, most have concluded that such monitoring is seldom necessary (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2013, 2019a, 2020; Magder, 2015).

69
00:09:14,404 --> 00:09:20,254
Formulas for deriving some hemodynamic parameters are shown in Table 50-2.

70
00:09:20,254 --> 00:09:25,816
These measurements can be indexed for body size by dividing by body surface area.

71
00:09:25,816 --> 00:09:32,229
Normal values for nonpregnant adults are used, but with the caveat that these may not necessarily

72
00:09:32,380 --> 00:09:37,430
reflect changes induced by the lowresistance uteroplacental circulation.

73
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:39,680
Table 50-2.

74
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:49,192
Formulas for Deriving Various Cardiopulmonary Parameters Cardiac complications are a common indication for ICU admission of pregnant women .

75
00:09:49,192 --> 00:09:54,917
Evaluation of cardiac function is frequently performed using echocardiography.

76
00:09:54,917 --> 00:10:06,080
This technology is indispensable in interrogating cardiac anatomy and especially rightventricular function (Krishnan, 2015; Thiele, 2015).

77
00:10:06,080 --> 00:10:15,767
It is considered in more detail in Chapter 52 (p. 918), and some normal values are listed in the Appendix (p. 1233).

78
00:10:15,767 --> 00:10:28,055
Given the association of cardiac dysfunction with maternal morbidity and mortality, efforts have strived to expand cardiac monitoring by including noninvasive cardiac output monitoring (NICOM).

79
00:10:28,055 --> 00:10:37,467
With this tool, a small electrical current is applied to the chest, and voltage signals are captured by four skin sensors arranged in a box on the chest.

80
00:10:37,467 --> 00:10:44,855
Compared with Doppler echocardiography, NICOM significantly overestimates stroke volume and cardiac output .

81
00:10:44,855 --> 00:10:50,580
This modality has not yet been widely adopted for use in the obstetrical population.

82
00:10:50,580 --> 00:10:53,180
Acute Pulmonary Edema.

83
00:10:53,180 --> 00:11:01,167
The incidence of pulmonary edema complicating pregnancy averages 1 in 500 deliveries at tertiary referral centers.

84
00:11:01,167 --> 00:11:10,542
Of the two general etiologies, cardiogenic, namely, hydrostatic edema is caused by high pulmonary capillary hydraulic pressures.

85
00:11:10,542 --> 00:11:19,267
The other is noncardiogenic, that is, permeability edema caused by capillary endothelial and alveolar epithelial damage.

86
00:11:19,267 --> 00:11:24,242
In pregnancy, noncardiogenic pulmonary edema is more common.

87
00:11:24,242 --> 00:11:41,130
More than half of pregnant women who develop pulmonary edema have some degree of sepsis in conjunction with tocolysis, severe preeclampsia, or obstetrical hemorrhage combined with vigorous fluid therapy (O’Dwyer, 2015; Pordeus, 2018).

88
00:11:41,130 --> 00:11:50,067
In one study, the etiologies of pulmonary edema were hypertension, cardiogenic, or both, and iatrogenic fluid overload .

89
00:11:50,067 --> 00:12:02,492
In another study of 53 cases, 83 percent were caused by hypertensive disorders; 11 percent, cardiac events; and 6 percent, sepsis (O’Dwyer, 2015).

90
00:12:02,492 --> 00:12:15,367
Although used less commonly today, tocolytic therapy with β-mimetic drugs was the cause of up to 40 percent of pulmonary edema cases (Gandhi, 2014; Jenkins, 2003).

91
00:12:15,367 --> 00:12:19,355
# Noncardiogenic Permeability Edema.

92
00:12:19,355 --> 00:12:33,192
Endotheliopathy is the common denominator that is associated with preeclampsia, sepsis, and acute hemorrhage—or frequently combinations thereof—and they are the most common predisposing factors to pulmonary edema .

93
00:12:33,192 --> 00:12:43,342
These clinical scenarios are often associated with corticosteroids given to induce fetal lung maturation along with vigorous fluid replacement and tocolytic therapy .

94
00:12:43,342 --> 00:12:48,105
Parenteral βagonists are indisputedly linked to pulmonary edema.

95
00:12:48,105 --> 00:12:59,642
Some studies have also associated magnesium sulfate given for preeclampsia (Gandhi, 2014; Wilson, 2014; Xiao, 2014).

96
00:12:59,642 --> 00:13:02,342
Combined therapy is causative.

97
00:13:02,342 --> 00:13:04,630
Table 50-3.

98
00:13:04,630 --> 00:13:12,492
Some Causes and Associated Factors for Pulmonary Edema in Pregnancy # Cardiogenic Hydrostatic Edema.

99
00:13:12,492 --> 00:13:26,492
Ventricular failure causing pulmonary edema in pregnancy is usually associated with some form of hypertension, hemorrhage and anemia, and sepsis (Cunningham, 2019; Vaught, 2018).

100
00:13:26,492 --> 00:13:39,867
Although it can be due to congenital or acquired anatomical defects, diastolic dysfunction is frequently from chronic hypertension, obesity, or both (Jessup, 2003; Kenchaiah, 2002).

101
00:13:39,867 --> 00:13:50,892
In these women, acute systolic hypertension exacerbates diastolic dysfunction and causes pulmonary edema (Dennis, 2012; Vaught, 2018).

102
00:13:50,892 --> 00:14:01,605
In two studies of pregnant women with preeclampsia and pulmonary edema, half of them were undelivered (Gandhi, 2014; Pordeus, 2018).

103
00:14:01,605 --> 00:14:04,005
# Diagnosis.

104
00:14:04,005 --> 00:14:08,167
Clinical examination is confirmed by chest radiographs.

105
00:14:08,167 --> 00:14:14,455
Bedside lung sonography may be helpful to detect fluid in the interstitial space and alveoli .

106
00:14:14,455 --> 00:14:22,067
In many cases, when echocardiography is done later, systolic function is normal as measured by ejection fraction.

107
00:14:22,067 --> 00:14:34,730
In addition, ventricular myocytes and fibroblasts secrete brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) and atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) with the distention seen in heart failure.

108
00:14:34,730 --> 00:14:38,280
In nonpregnant patients, concentrations

109
00:14:38,428 --> 00:14:49,690
<100 pg/mL have an excellent negative predictive value, and levels >500 pg/mL have an excellent positive predictive value.

110
00:14:49,640 --> 00:14:57,103
It is problematic that levels frequently are 100 to 500 pg/mL and thus are nondiagnostic.

111
00:14:57,103 --> 00:15:08,015
Moreover, concentrations of N-terminal BNP and ANP are both elevated with preeclampsia (Szabo, 2014; Tihtonen, 2007).

112
00:15:08,015 --> 00:15:17,465
Normal values are discussed in Chapter 4 (p. 65), and those for normal pregnancy are given in the Appendix (p. 1231).

113
00:15:17,465 --> 00:15:19,690
# Management.

114
00:15:19,690 --> 00:15:23,753
Acute pulmonary edema requires emergency care.

115
00:15:23,753 --> 00:15:32,178
Oxygen therapy is initiated and furosemide is given in periodic 20- to 40-mg intravenous doses as needed.

116
00:15:32,178 --> 00:15:39,590
For oxygen delivery, nasal cannula, simple masks, and nonrebreathing masks are first-line systems.

117
00:15:39,590 --> 00:15:45,565
Both nasal cannula and simple mask allow mixing of supplemental oxygen with room air.

118
00:15:45,565 --> 00:15:49,765
Consequently, oxygen administration is imprecise .

119
00:15:49,765 --> 00:15:56,590
A nonrebreathing mask has valves that limit the mixing of exhaled gas and room air with the oxygen supply.

120
00:15:56,590 --> 00:16:04,565
Flow rates up to 10 to 15 L/min may be achieved, and oxygen concentrations approach 95 percent .

121
00:16:04,565 --> 00:16:11,528
As a treatment goal, a pulse oximetry reading >92 percent reflects adequate oxygenation.

122
00:16:11,528 --> 00:16:16,015
Further treatment depends on whether a woman is ante- or postpartum.

123
00:16:16,015 --> 00:16:25,840
A live fetus prohibits the use of cardioactive drugs that might rapidly lower peripheral resistance and in turn severely diminish uteroplacental circulation.

124
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:32,915
The cause of cardiogenic failure is determined by echocardiography, which will help direct further therapy.

125
00:16:32,915 --> 00:16:39,578
Importantly, acute pulmonary edema is not an indication for emergency cesarean delivery.

126
00:16:39,578 --> 00:16:42,528
Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome.

127
00:16:42,528 --> 00:16:52,828
Acute lung injury that causes a form of severe permeability pulmonary edema and respiratory failure is termed acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).

128
00:16:52,828 --> 00:17:03,003
This is a pathophysiological continuum from mild pulmonary insufficiency to dependence on high inspired oxygen concentrations and mechanical ventilation.

129
00:17:03,003 --> 00:17:10,365
Uniform criteria for its diagnosis are lacking, and thus the incidence is variably reported for pregnancy.

130
00:17:10,365 --> 00:17:23,365
Rush and colleagues (2017) queried the nationwide inpatient samples of more than 5.4 million pregnancies and cited an incidence of 60 ventilated cases per 100,000 births.

131
00:17:23,365 --> 00:17:27,140
They found an overall mortality rate of 9 percent.

132
00:17:27,140 --> 00:17:31,515
This rate is much higher if caused or complicated by sepsis.

133
00:17:31,515 --> 00:17:38,303
Last, if ARDS develops antepartum, the perinatal mortality rate is correspondingly high.

134
00:17:38,303 --> 00:17:40,653
# Definitions.

135
00:17:40,653 --> 00:17:46,940
Physiological criteria required for diagnosis of acute lung injury are study dependent.

136
00:17:46,940 --> 00:17:51,215
In general, less precise terms are used for clinical care.

137
00:17:51,215 --> 00:17:56,953
Clinical findings include dyspnea, tachypnea, and oxygen desaturation.

138
00:17:56,953 --> 00:18:13,215
Most investigators define ARDS as radiographically documented pulmonary infiltrates, a ratio of arterial oxygen tension to the fraction of inspired oxygen (PaO2:FiO2) <200, and no evidence of heart failure .

139
00:18:13,215 --> 00:18:21,053
Revised by international consensus, the Berlin Definition includes categories of mild, moderate, and severe .

140
00:18:21,053 --> 00:18:23,990
# Etiopathogenesis.

141
00:18:23,990 --> 00:18:28,628
ARDS begins with an acute lung injury from various causes .

142
00:18:28,628 --> 00:18:36,353
More than half of pregnant women with ARDS have some combination of sepsis, hemorrhage, shock, and fluid overload.

143
00:18:36,353 --> 00:18:42,165
Diffuse infectious pneumonia and sepsis are two of the most frequent single-agent causes.

144
00:18:42,165 --> 00:18:46,803
Because it is common, influenza pneumonitis often is a cause.

145
00:18:46,803 --> 00:18:57,553
Other etiologies such as pneumococcal, metapneumovirus, and Legionnaire disease also are encountered (Close, 2016; Fuchs, 2017).

146
00:18:57,553 --> 00:19:05,378
Of sepsis causes, pyelonephritis, puerperal pelvic infection, and chorioamnionitis are the most frequent.

147
00:19:05,378 --> 00:19:11,128
The contribution of transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) is unclear .

148
00:19:11,128 --> 00:19:13,390
Table 50-4.

149
00:19:13,390 --> 00:19:23,990
Some Causes of Acute Lung Injury and Respiratory Failure Endothelial injury in the lung capillaries releases cytokines that recruit neutrophils to the inflammation site.

150
00:19:23,990 --> 00:19:28,478
Here, they elaborate more cytokines to worsen tissue injury.

151
00:19:28,478 --> 00:19:32,015
Three stages describe ARDS development .

152
00:19:32,015 --> 00:19:42,440
First, the exudative phase follows widespread injury to the alveolar epithelium and to the microvascular endothelium, including the pulmonary vasculature.

153
00:19:42,440 --> 00:19:45,503
This endotheliopathy results in

154
00:19:45,652 --> 00:19:56,764
increased pulmonary capillary permeability, surfactant loss or inactivation, diminished lung volume, and vascular shunting that creates arterial hypoxemia.

155
00:19:56,714 --> 00:20:04,089
Next, the proliferative phase usually begins approximately 7 days later and lasts up to 21 days.

156
00:20:04,089 --> 00:20:07,289
Most patients recover during this phase.

157
00:20:07,289 --> 00:20:10,927
Last, the fibrotic phase results from healing.

158
00:20:10,927 --> 00:20:20,802
Despite these insults, the long-term prognosis for pulmonary function is surprisingly good (Baron, 2018; Herridge, 2003).

159
00:20:20,802 --> 00:20:23,277
# Clinical Course.

160
00:20:23,277 --> 00:20:32,377
With pulmonary injury, the clinical condition depends largely on the insult magnitude, the ability to compensate for it, and the disease stage.

161
00:20:32,377 --> 00:20:41,664
At first, physical examination findings are absent, except perhaps hyperventilation, and arterial oxygenation usually is adequate.

162
00:20:41,664 --> 00:20:52,139
With worsening, clinical and radiological evidence for pulmonary edema, decreased lung compliance, and increased intrapulmonary blood shunting become apparent.

163
00:20:52,139 --> 00:20:59,752
Progressive alveolar and interstitial edema develops, and inflammatory cells and erythrocytes extravasate.

164
00:20:59,752 --> 00:21:08,064
Ideally, pulmonary injury is identified at this early stage, and specific therapy is directed at the underlying insult.

165
00:21:08,064 --> 00:21:15,789
Further progression to acute respiratory failure is characterized by marked dyspnea, tachypnea, and hypoxemia.

166
00:21:15,789 --> 00:21:22,302
Additional functional lung volume loss results in worsening of pulmonary compliance and greater shunting.

167
00:21:22,302 --> 00:21:30,864
Diffuse abnormalities are heard by auscultation, and a chest radiograph characteristically demonstrates bilateral lung involvement .

168
00:21:30,864 --> 00:21:36,477
At this phase, the injury ordinarily would be lethal without ventilatory support.

169
00:21:36,477 --> 00:21:42,064
When shunting exceeds 30 percent, severe refractory hypoxemia develops.

170
00:21:42,064 --> 00:21:50,427
Comorbid metabolic and respiratory acidosis can result in myocardial irritability, dysfunction, and cardiac arrest.

171
00:21:50,427 --> 00:21:52,789
Figure 50-1.

172
00:21:52,789 --> 00:22:04,739
Anteroposterior chest radiograph of a second-trimester pregnant woman with marked bilateral parenchymal and pleural opacification secondary to acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS).

173
00:22:04,739 --> 00:22:06,964
# Management.

174
00:22:06,964 --> 00:22:16,252
ARDS mortality rates have declined because of advances in critical care medicine (Baron, 2018; Fan, 2018).

175
00:22:16,252 --> 00:22:23,239
Management requires close attention to recognizing and treating underlying medical and surgical disorders.

176
00:22:23,239 --> 00:22:33,964
In cases of severe acute lung injury, providing adequate oxygenation of peripheral tissues is balanced against ventilatory maneuvers that further aggravate lung injury.

177
00:22:33,964 --> 00:22:42,539
At least intuitively, increasing oxygen delivery should produce a corresponding rise in tissue uptake, but this is difficult to measure.

178
00:22:42,539 --> 00:22:47,927
Support of systemic perfusion with intravenous crystalloid and blood is imperative.

179
00:22:47,927 --> 00:22:57,514
Because sepsis is commonplace in lung injury, aggressive antimicrobial therapy is given for infection, and any necrotic tissues are debrided.

180
00:22:57,514 --> 00:23:01,989
Oxygen delivery can be greatly improved by correcting anemia.

181
00:23:01,989 --> 00:23:09,802
Specifically, each gram of hemoglobin carries 1.25 mL of oxygen when 90-percent saturated.

182
00:23:09,802 --> 00:23:23,202
By comparison, raising the arterial PaO2 from 100 to 200 mm Hg results in the transport of only 0.1 mL of additional oxygen for each 100 mL of blood.

183
00:23:23,202 --> 00:23:40,477
Reasonable goals in caring for the woman with severe lung injury are a PaO2 of 60 mm Hg or 90-percent oxygen saturation using an inspired oxygen content <50 percent and positive end-expiratory pressures <15 mm Hg.

184
00:23:40,477 --> 00:23:47,714
With regard to the pregnancy, it remains controversial whether delivery of the fetus improves maternal oxygenation .

185
00:23:47,714 --> 00:23:54,102
In a study of 29 women undergoing mechanical ventilation, 10 were delivered while intubated .

186
00:23:54,102 --> 00:24:01,264
Respiratory function modestly improved in half, but no factors were identified that predicted a better outcome.

187
00:24:01,264 --> 00:24:04,114
# Mechanical Ventilation.

188
00:24:04,114 --> 00:24:14,289
Noninvasive ventilation, that is, positive-pressure ventilation by face mask, may be effective in some women in early stages of pulmonary insufficiency .

189
00:24:14,289 --> 00:24:18,264
Conventional ventilation is done with tracheal intubation.

190
00:24:18,264 --> 00:24:26,052
Early intubation is preferred in the gravida if respiratory failure is more likely than not and especially if it appears imminent.

191
00:24:26,052 --> 00:24:36,402
Because of the physiological changes outlined in Chapter 4 (p. 67), PaCO2 is normally reduced in a gravida relative to a nonpregnant woman.

192
00:24:36,402 --> 00:24:37,852
Thus,

193
00:24:38,020 --> 00:24:49,170
intubation should be considered when PaCO2 levels reach 35 to 40 mm Hg. Mechanical ventilation is used to assist or replace spontaneous respiration .

194
00:24:49,120 --> 00:24:55,432
It improves oxygenation by its application of highoxygen-content gas and positive pressure.

195
00:24:55,432 --> 00:25:09,782
Many successful formulas for mechanical ventilation are employed, and initially a tidal volume ≤6 mL/kg is optimal (Fan, 2018; Schwaiberger, 2016).

196
00:25:09,782 --> 00:25:23,845
Adjustments are made to obtain a PaO2 >60 mm Hg or a hemoglobin oxygen saturation ≥90 percent and to reach a PaCO2 of 35 to 45 mm Hg.

197
00:25:23,845 --> 00:25:29,182
Lower PaO2 levels are avoided, because placental perfusion may be impaired.

198
00:25:29,182 --> 00:25:34,595
The mode is the manner in which ventilator breaths are triggered, cycled, and limited.

199
00:25:34,595 --> 00:25:42,357
Three commonly used ventilation modes of include assistcontrol, intermittent mandatory, and pressure-support ventilation.

200
00:25:42,357 --> 00:25:52,420
Although not a support mode, continuous positive airway pressure can be used in patients with an intact ventilatory system who require little ventilatory support.

201
00:25:52,420 --> 00:26:01,620
Pregnancy In a study of 51 women with ARDS, almost half had severe preeclampsia, and most required intubation postpartum.

202
00:26:01,620 --> 00:26:07,295
Eleven were delivered while being ventilated, and another six were discharged undelivered .

203
00:26:07,295 --> 00:26:13,432
Two mothers died, and this included a woman who died as a complication of tocolytic treatment.

204
00:26:13,432 --> 00:26:24,045
In a multicenter report, Lapinsky and associates (2015) described 29 pregnant women undergoing mechanical ventilation for various indications.

205
00:26:24,045 --> 00:26:29,332
Only a few had severe ARDS, and the mortality rate was 10 percent.

206
00:26:29,332 --> 00:26:36,032
Ten women delivered while being ventilated, but this had little beneficial effect on their respiratory status.

207
00:26:36,032 --> 00:26:49,932
Positive End-Expiratory Pressure With severe lung injury and high intrapulmonary shunt fractions, it may not be possible to provide adequate oxygenation with usual ventilatory pressures, even with 100-percent oxygen.

208
00:26:49,932 --> 00:26:57,057
Positive end-expiratory pressure is usually successful in decreasing the shunt by recruiting collapsed alveoli.

209
00:26:57,057 --> 00:27:03,945
At low levels of 5 to 15 mm Hg, positive pressure can typically be used safely.

210
00:27:03,945 --> 00:27:20,757
At higher levels, impaired right-sided venous return can result in decreased cardiac output, lowered uteroplacental perfusion, alveolar overdistention, falling compliance, and barotrauma (Celli, 2018; Schwaiberger, 2016).

211
00:27:20,757 --> 00:27:34,995
Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation As discussed in Chapter 33 (p. 600), extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) has been successfully used for neonatal meconium aspiration syndrome.

212
00:27:34,995 --> 00:27:47,895
Some preliminary observations suggest that veno-venous ECMO may be useful in adults with ARDS (Combes, 2018; Ende, 2016; Matthay, 2018).

213
00:27:47,895 --> 00:27:59,682
In one case series, 12 pregnant women with influenza-induced lung failure were treated with ECMO, and of four maternal deaths, three were due to anticoagulation-related hemorrhage .

214
00:27:59,682 --> 00:28:08,432
ECMO use also has been reported for status asthmaticus (Clifford, 2018; Steinack, 2017).

215
00:28:08,432 --> 00:28:22,857
In multiple reviews, ECMO was used mostly for ARDS, and the maternal and perinatal mortality rate approximated 30 percent (Liu, 2018; Ramanathan, 2020; Saad, 2016).

216
00:28:22,857 --> 00:28:29,870
Maternal survival appears to be significantly lower compared with nonpregnant women requiring ECMO .

217
00:28:29,870 --> 00:28:38,482
Fetal Oxygenation The propensity of the hemoglobin molecule to release oxygen is described by the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve .

218
00:28:38,482 --> 00:28:51,932
For clinical purposes, the curve can be divided into an upper oxygen association curve representing the alveolar-capillary environment and a lower oxygen dissociation portion representing the tissue-capillary environment.

219
00:28:51,932 --> 00:28:57,970
Shifts of the curve have their greatest effect at the steep portion because they affect oxygen delivery.

220
00:28:57,970 --> 00:29:06,382
A rightward shift is associated with decreased hemoglobin affinity for oxygen and hence greater tissue-capillary oxygen interchange.

221
00:29:06,382 --> 00:29:14,707
Hypercapnia, metabolic acidosis, fever, and increased 2,3-diphosphoglycerate levels produce rightward shifts.

222
00:29:14,707 --> 00:29:25,020
During normal pregnancy, the erythrocyte concentration of 2,3-diphosphoglycerate rises by approximately 30 percent, which favors oxygen delivery.

223
00:29:25,020 --> 00:29:27,282
Figure 50-2.

224
00:29:27,282 --> 00:29:30,532
Oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve.

225
00:29:30,532 --> 00:29:37,595
With higher oxygen tension (PaO2) in the pulmonary alveoli, adult hemoglobin is maximally

226
00:29:37,756 --> 00:29:43,193
saturated compared with that at the lower oxygen tension in the tissue capillaries.

227
00:29:43,143 --> 00:29:52,493
Note that at any given oxygen tension, fetal hemoglobin carries more oxygen than adult hemoglobin, as indicated by percent saturation.

228
00:29:52,493 --> 00:29:57,418
Fetal hemoglobin has a higher oxygen affinity than adult hemoglobin.

229
00:29:57,418 --> 00:30:05,018
As seen in Figure 50-2, its oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve is positioned to the left of the adult curve.

230
00:30:05,018 --> 00:30:16,056
To achieve 50-percent hemoglobin saturation in the mother, the PaO2 must be 27 mm Hg compared with only 19 mm Hg in the fetus.

231
00:30:16,056 --> 00:30:23,781
Under normal physiological conditions, the fetus is constantly on the dissociation, or tissue, portion of the curve.

232
00:30:23,781 --> 00:30:31,518
Even with severe maternal lung disease and very low PaO2 levels, oxygen displacement to fetal tissues is favored.

233
00:30:31,518 --> 00:30:43,343
For example, in pregnant women who live at high altitudes, and despite a maternal PaO2 of only 60 mm Hg, the fetal PaO2 is equivalent to that seen at sea level .

234
00:30:43,343 --> 00:30:53,218
Intravenous Fluids Although mortality rates are similar, conservative versus liberal fluid management is associated with fewer days of mechanical ventilation .

235
00:30:53,218 --> 00:31:01,781
Some normal pregnancy-induced physiological changes predispose to a greater risk of permeability edema from vigorous fluid therapy.

236
00:31:01,781 --> 00:31:12,631
Serum albumin concentration determines colloid oncotic pressure (COP), and 1 g/dL exerts approximately 6 mm Hg of pressure.

237
00:31:12,631 --> 00:31:20,006
As discussed in Chapter 4 (p. 70), serum albumin concentrations normally drop in pregnancy.

238
00:31:20,006 --> 00:31:31,593
This results in a decline in COP from 28 mm Hg in the nonpregnant woman to 23 mm Hg at term and to 17 mm Hg in the puerperium .

239
00:31:31,593 --> 00:31:39,306
With preeclampsia, endotheliopathy causes extravascular albumin loss and lowered serum albumin levels.

240
00:31:39,306 --> 00:31:46,206
These changes have a significant clinical effect on the colloid oncotic pressure/wedge pressure gradient.

241
00:31:46,206 --> 00:31:50,618
Normally, this gradient exceeds 8 mm Hg.

242
00:31:50,618 --> 00:31:59,143
However, when it is ≤4 mm Hg, the risk for pulmonary edema rises due to Starling forces.

243
00:31:59,143 --> 00:32:05,181
Finally, no benefits are gained by albumin versus crystalloid infusions in these women .

244
00:32:05,181 --> 00:32:07,893
# Long-Term Outcomes.

245
00:32:07,893 --> 00:32:12,443
Most patients with ARDS regain nearly normal lung function .

246
00:32:12,443 --> 00:32:20,318
In nonpregnant subjects, however, risks for impaired global cognitive function at 3 and 12 months are significant .

247
00:32:20,318 --> 00:32:25,693
Moreover, return to basic normal activity is delayed 1 to 2 years.

248
00:32:25,693 --> 00:32:37,743
In a 5-year follow-up study, Herridge and coworkers (2011) reported normal lung function but significant exercise limitation as well as physical and psychological sequelae.

249
00:32:37,743 --> 00:32:43,206
Long-term follow-up studies of pregnant women who recover from ARDS are lacking.

250
00:32:43,206 --> 00:32:45,756
Sepsis And Septic Shock.

251
00:32:45,756 --> 00:32:53,368
Sepsis is defined as infection with organ dysfunction, and septic shock manifests with unresponsive hypotension .

252
00:32:53,368 --> 00:33:03,831
The Third International Consensus Definition—“Sepsis-3”—denotes clinical criteria for sepsis to include suspected infection and acute organ dysfunction .

253
00:33:03,831 --> 00:33:11,556
Use of the terms systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS) and severe sepsis are no longer recommended.

254
00:33:11,556 --> 00:33:21,056
The syndrome is induced by a systemic inflammatory response to bacteria or viruses or their by-products such as endotoxins or exotoxins.

255
00:33:21,056 --> 00:33:23,656
Its severity is a continuum .

256
00:33:23,656 --> 00:33:34,293
In an analysis of 55 million births, Kendle and associates (2019) found an incidence of 2.4 cases per 10,000 deliveries.

257
00:33:34,293 --> 00:33:41,268
The Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine cites the incidence to be 4 to 10 cases per 10,000 births.

258
00:33:41,268 --> 00:33:53,818
According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), infection and sepsis caused 12 percent of pregnancyrelated deaths in the United States from 2013 to 2017 .

259
00:33:53,818 --> 00:33:56,181
Figure 50-3.

260
00:33:56,181 --> 00:34:02,731
Sepsis begins with a systemic inflammatory response to infection that may progress to septic shock.

261
00:34:02,731 --> 00:34:15,118
Infections that most commonly cause sepsis syndrome in obstetrics are pyelonephritis , chorioamnionitis and puerperal sepsis , pneumonia , septic abortion , and necrotizing fasciitis .

262
00:34:15,118 --> 00:34:21,218
Septic shock is defined by the need for vasopressor therapy despite fluid resuscitation.

263
00:34:21,218 --> 00:34:25,843
The mortality rate for this in nonpregnant patients is 20 percent .

264
00:34:25,843 --> 00:34:34,893
The maternal mortality risk from sepsis is significantly underestimated (Bauer, 2015; Chebbo, 2016).

265
00:34:34,893 --> 00:34:39,581
However, most evidence indicates that case-fatality rates are

266
00:34:39,748 --> 00:34:44,635
lower for pregnant women compared with age-matched nonpregnant controls .

267
00:34:44,585 --> 00:34:47,523
# Etiopathogenesis.

268
00:34:47,523 --> 00:34:58,285
Most information concerning sepsis pathogenesis derives from study of lipopolysaccharide (LPS), which is the endotoxin portion of gram-negative bacteria.

269
00:34:58,285 --> 00:35:09,260
The lipid A moiety is bound by mononuclear blood cells, becomes internalized, and stimulates release of mediators and a series of complex downstream perturbations.

270
00:35:09,260 --> 00:35:21,460
Clinical aspects of sepsis manifest when cytokines that have endocrine, paracrine, and autocrine actions are released (Seymour, 2018; Suffredini, 1989).

271
00:35:21,460 --> 00:35:28,385
Although sepsis in obstetrics may be caused by several pathogens, most cases stem from a small group.

272
00:35:28,385 --> 00:35:40,610
Sepsis and bacteremia complicating pyelonephritis are commonly caused by Escherichia coli and Klebsiella species (Cunningham, 1987; Snyder, 2013).

273
00:35:40,610 --> 00:35:52,148
Although pelvic infections are usually polymicrobial, bacteria that cause severe sepsis are frequently endotoxin-producing Enterobacteriaceae, and most commonly E coli .

274
00:35:52,148 --> 00:35:59,910
Other pelvic pathogens are aerobic and anaerobic streptococci, Bacteroides species, and Clostridium species.

275
00:35:59,910 --> 00:36:05,423
Of these, group A streptococcal infections are a significant source of infection .

276
00:36:05,423 --> 00:36:21,823
Some strains of group A β-hemolytic streptococci and Staphylococcus aureus—including community-acquired methicillin-resistant S aureus strains (CA-MRSA)—produce a superantigen that activates T cells to rapidly cause all features of sepsis.

277
00:36:21,823 --> 00:36:33,210
This manifests as toxic shock syndrome, which is discussed further in Chapter 37 (p. 659) (Moellering, 2011; Soper, 2011).

278
00:36:33,210 --> 00:36:38,198
Potent bacterial exotoxins also can cause severe sepsis syndrome.

279
00:36:38,198 --> 00:36:57,460
Examples include exotoxins from Clostridium perfringens or sordellii, toxic shock syndrome toxin 1 (TSST-1) from S aureus, and toxic shock–like exotoxin from group A β-hemolytic streptococci (Herrera, 2016; Kaiser, 2018).

280
00:36:57,460 --> 00:37:09,435
These last exotoxins cause rapid and extensive tissue necrosis and gangrene, especially of the postpartum uterus, and may cause profound cardiovascular collapse and maternal death.

281
00:37:09,435 --> 00:37:16,085
In a review discussed subsequently, the maternal mortality rate from these infections was 58 percent .

282
00:37:16,085 --> 00:37:23,560
Sepsis begins with an inflammatory response that is directed against microbial endotoxins and exotoxins.

283
00:37:23,560 --> 00:37:40,985
CD4 T cells and leukocytes are stimulated to produce proinflammatory compounds that include tumor necrosis factor α (TNF-α), several interleukins, and other cytokines, proteases, oxidants, and bradykinin that result in a “cytokine storm.

284
00:37:40,985 --> 00:37:53,798
Many other cellular reactions then follow and include stimulation of pro- and antiinflammatory compounds, procoagulant activity, gene activation, receptor regulation, and immune suppression .

285
00:37:53,798 --> 00:38:00,023
It is also likely that interleukin 6 (IL-6) mediates myocardial suppression .

286
00:38:00,023 --> 00:38:07,160
The pathophysiological response to this cascade is selective vasodilation with maldistribution of blood flow.

287
00:38:07,160 --> 00:38:11,423
Leukocyte and platelet aggregation cause capillary plugging.

288
00:38:11,423 --> 00:38:19,373
Worsening endotheliopathy causes profound permeability, capillary leakage, and interstitial fluid accumulation.

289
00:38:19,373 --> 00:38:30,035
Depending on the degree of endothelial injury and inflammatory response, a pathophysiological and clinical continuum evolves as depicted in Figure 50-3.

290
00:38:30,035 --> 00:38:36,535
The clinical syndrome begins with subtle signs of sepsis from infection and terminates with septic shock.

291
00:38:36,535 --> 00:38:46,735
In its early stages, clinical shock results primarily from lowered systemic vascular resistance that is not compensated fully by increased cardiac output.

292
00:38:46,735 --> 00:38:57,273
Hypoperfusion worsens lactic acidosis, decreases tissue oxygen extraction, and leads to end-organ dysfunction that includes acute lung and kidney injury.

293
00:38:57,273 --> 00:39:07,010
Finally, septic cardiomyopathy with global involvement is common with severe sepsis (Beesley, 2018; Martin, 2018).

294
00:39:07,010 --> 00:39:10,098
# Clinical Manifestations.

295
00:39:10,098 --> 00:39:20,848
Sepsis has myriad clinical manifestations that, at least in part, are dependent on the specific invading microorganism and its particular endo- or exotoxins.

296
00:39:20,848 --> 00:39:25,960
Some of the general effects of LPS are shown in Table 50-5.

297
00:39:25,960 --> 00:39:28,385
Table 50-5.

298
00:39:28,385 --> 00:39:37,385
Clinical Manifestations of Sepsis Thus, although capillary leakage initially causes hypovolemia, if intravenous crystalloid is given

299
00:39:37,540 --> 00:39:45,665
at this point, sepsis hemodynamically can be described as a high-cardiac-output, low-systemic-vascular-resistance condition .

300
00:39:45,615 --> 00:39:55,265
Concomitantly, pulmonary hypertension develops, and despite the high cardiac output, severe sepsis also causes myocardial depression .

301
00:39:55,265 --> 00:39:59,577
This is often referred to as the warm phase of septic shock.

302
00:39:59,577 --> 00:40:11,077
These findings are the most common cardiovascular manifestations of early sepsis, but they can be accompanied by some of the other clinical or laboratory aberrations listed in the prior section.

303
00:40:11,077 --> 00:40:13,440
Figure 50-4.

304
00:40:13,440 --> 00:40:16,402
Hemodynamic effects of sepsis.

305
00:40:16,402 --> 00:40:20,265
Values for normal women at term are shown by dots.

306
00:40:20,265 --> 00:40:25,790
With early sepsis, there is high cardiac output and low vascular resistance.

307
00:40:25,790 --> 00:40:33,227
With fluid resuscitation, cardiac output increases even more, but so does capillary hydraulic pressure.

308
00:40:33,227 --> 00:40:40,365
With continued sepsis, there may be myocardial depression to further increase capillary hydraulic pressure.

309
00:40:40,365 --> 00:40:53,065
Decreased plasma oncotic pressure (serum albumin [g] × 6 mm Hg) contributes to interstitial lung fluid and endo/epithelial leak causes alveolar flooding.

310
00:40:53,065 --> 00:41:03,452
IV = intravenous; LVSWI = left ventricular stroke work index; PCWP = pulmonary capillary wedge pressure.

311
00:41:03,452 --> 00:41:08,127
The response to initial intravenous hydration may be prognostic.

312
00:41:08,127 --> 00:41:18,665
Most pregnant women who have early sepsis show a salutary response with crystalloid and antimicrobial therapy, and if indicated, debridement of infected tissue.

313
00:41:18,665 --> 00:41:25,802
Conversely, if following vigorous fluid infusion, septic shock results, then the prognosis is more guarded.

314
00:41:25,802 --> 00:41:42,102
At this juncture, if β-adrenergic inotropic agents fail to improve cardiovascular status, this indicates severe and unresponsive extracellular fluid extravasation with vascular insufficiency, overwhelming myocardial depression, or both.

315
00:41:42,102 --> 00:41:50,727
Oliguria and continued peripheral vasoconstriction characterize a secondary, cold phase of septic shock that is rarely survived.

316
00:41:50,727 --> 00:42:02,915
Another poor prognostic sign is continued renal, pulmonary, and cerebral dysfunction once hypotension is corrected (Chebbo, 2016; Seymour, 2018).

317
00:42:02,915 --> 00:42:09,127
The average risk of death rises by 15 to 20 percent with failure of each organ system.

318
00:42:09,127 --> 00:42:11,352
# Management.

319
00:42:11,352 --> 00:42:17,952
In 2004, an international consensus effort was launched as the Surviving Sepsis Campaign .

320
00:42:17,952 --> 00:42:30,290
The cornerstone of management was termed early goal-directed therapy (EGDT), which stressed prompt recognition of serious bacterial infection and close monitoring of vital signs and urine flow.

321
00:42:30,290 --> 00:42:43,165
After three multicenter trials did not show that this protocol improved survival rates, this strategy is no longer recommended (PRISM Investigators, 2017; Seymour, 2018).

322
00:42:43,165 --> 00:42:52,340
Similar conclusions were reached with sets of early warning systems in obstetrics (Edwards, 2015; Mhyre, 2014).

323
00:42:52,340 --> 00:43:02,152
Modified Early Warning Score (MEWS) failed to predict sepsis in obstetrical populations with positive predictive values as low as 1 to 2 percent .

324
00:43:02,152 --> 00:43:15,952
Reasons for such poor performance in pregnant women include physiological changes of vital signs; relatively fewer comorbidities in a younger, healthier population; and predominantly genitourinary infections.

325
00:43:15,952 --> 00:43:25,940
Thus, current efforts such as the Sepsis in Obstetrics Score have been designed to identify women who benefit from ICU admission early in their hospital course .

326
00:43:25,940 --> 00:43:32,577
However, the validity of these findings beyond a single center and against controls is yet unstudied.

327
00:43:32,577 --> 00:43:37,715
Still, the major tenets of EGDT are employed for sepsis treatment.

328
00:43:37,715 --> 00:43:51,377
Management of sepsis stresses prompt administration of broad-spectrum antimicrobials, collection of specimens for culture, serum lactate level measurement, an intravenous fluid bolus, and vasopressors as needed .

329
00:43:51,377 --> 00:43:56,577
An algorithm for management of sepsis is shown in Figure 50-5.

330
00:43:56,577 --> 00:44:08,252
The three basic steps are performed as simultaneously as possible and include evaluation of the sepsis source and its sequelae, cardiopulmonary function assessment, and immediate management.

331
00:44:08,252 --> 00:44:19,615
The most important step in sepsis management is rapid infusion of 2 L and sometimes as many as 4 to 6 L of crystalloid fluids to restore renal perfusion in severely affected women .

332
00:44:19,615 --> 00:44:25,365
Simultaneously, appropriately chosen broad-spectrum antimicrobials are begun,

333
00:44:25,516 --> 00:44:33,703
preferentially within 1 hour (Chen, 2019; Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, 2019).

334
00:44:33,653 --> 00:44:40,403
Because hemoconcentration is caused by the capillary leak, if anemia coexists, blood is given.

335
00:44:40,403 --> 00:44:43,803
Specific thresholds for gravidas are lacking.

336
00:44:43,803 --> 00:44:58,666
In a group of nonpregnant individuals admitted to an ICU, maintaining the hemoglobin concentration ≥9 g/dL did not have superior outcomes compared with levels ≥7 g/dL .

337
00:44:58,666 --> 00:45:06,753
That said, fetal oxygenation is improved by the higher concentration, as discussed earlier (p. 886).

338
00:45:06,753 --> 00:45:09,241
Figure 50-5.

339
00:45:09,241 --> 00:45:13,253
Algorithm for evaluation and management of sepsis.

340
00:45:13,253 --> 00:45:17,641
Rapid and aggressive implementation is paramount for success.

341
00:45:17,641 --> 00:45:24,391
The three steps—Evaluate, Assess, and Manage—are carried out as simultaneously as possible.

342
00:45:24,391 --> 00:45:28,903
The use of a colloid solution such as hetastarch is controversial .

343
00:45:28,903 --> 00:45:38,666
One Scandinavian randomized trial comparing hydroxyethyl starch and Ringer acetate solutions reported a higher mortality rate with the starch solution .

344
00:45:38,666 --> 00:45:45,428
Another study found equivalent results with 6-percent hydroxyethyl starch compared with normal saline .

345
00:45:45,428 --> 00:45:50,191
Additionally, albumin was not found to be superior to crystalloids .

346
00:45:50,191 --> 00:46:01,691
Aggressive volume replacement ideally is promptly followed by urinary output of at least 30 and preferably 50 mL/h, as well as other indicators of improved perfusion.

347
00:46:01,691 --> 00:46:05,741
If not, vasoactive drug therapy is considered .

348
00:46:05,741 --> 00:46:12,916
As discussed, mortality rates are high when sepsis is further complicated by respiratory or renal failure.

349
00:46:12,916 --> 00:46:22,241
With severe sepsis, damage to pulmonary capillary endothelium and alveolar epithelium causes alveolar flooding and pulmonary edema.

350
00:46:22,241 --> 00:46:30,278
This may occur even with low or normal pulmonary capillary wedge pressures, as with the ARDS (p. 884).

351
00:46:30,278 --> 00:46:35,066
Therefore, in some women, mechanical ventilation is indicated.

352
00:46:35,066 --> 00:46:41,191
Broad-spectrum antimicrobials are chosen empirically based on the probable source of infection.

353
00:46:41,191 --> 00:46:50,478
They are given promptly in maximal doses after appropriate cultures are taken of blood, urine, or exudates not contaminated by normal flora.

354
00:46:50,478 --> 00:47:00,103
In severe sepsis, appropriate and early empirical coverage results in better survival rates (Seymour, 2017, 2018).

355
00:47:00,103 --> 00:47:08,953
Acute pyelonephritis is usually caused by Enterobacteriaceae, as discussed in Chapter 56 (p. 997).

356
00:47:08,953 --> 00:47:21,228
For pelvic infections, empirical selection of a regimen such as ampicillin, gentamicin, plus clindamycin, which in sum covers aerobic and anaerobic pathogens, generally suffices .

357
00:47:21,228 --> 00:47:30,378
Associated incisional and other soft-tissue infections are increasingly likely to be caused by MRSA, thus vancomycin may be added .

358
00:47:30,378 --> 00:47:40,391
With septic abortion or deep fascial infections, a Gram-stained smear may be helpful in identifying Clostridium species or group A streptococcal organisms.

359
00:47:40,391 --> 00:47:49,153
Surgical Treatment Continuing sepsis may prove fatal, and debridement of necrotic tissue or drainage of purulent material is crucial .

360
00:47:49,153 --> 00:47:58,016
Puerperal pelvic infections include metritis and infections of perineal lacerations or of hysterotomy or laparotomy incisions.

361
00:47:58,016 --> 00:48:02,291
If complicated by abscess, these may require drainage.

362
00:48:02,291 --> 00:48:11,491
With a septic abortion, uterine contents must be removed expeditiously by curettage as described in Chapter 11 (p. 203).

363
00:48:11,491 --> 00:48:15,991
Hysterectomy is seldom indicated unless gangrene has resulted.

364
00:48:15,991 --> 00:48:26,603
For women with pyelonephritis, continuing sepsis should prompt a search for obstruction caused by calculi or by a perinephric or intrarenal phlegmon or abscess.

365
00:48:26,603 --> 00:48:32,703
Renal sonography or “one-shot” pyelography can help diagnose obstruction and calculi.

366
00:48:32,703 --> 00:48:40,466
With obstruction, ureteral catheterization, percutaneous nephrostomy, or flank exploration may be lifesaving .

367
00:48:40,466 --> 00:48:48,428
Computed tomography (CT) or magnetic-resonance imaging aids identification of a renal phlegmon or abscess.

368
00:48:48,428 --> 00:48:50,691
Puerperal Infections.

369
00:48:50,691 --> 00:49:02,803
Most cases of puerperal pelvic sepsis are clinically manifested in the first several postpartum days, and intravenous antimicrobial therapy without tissue debridement is generally curative.

370
00:49:02,803 --> 00:49:05,666
At least four exceptions are notable.

371
00:49:05,666 --> 00:49:17,203
First, massive uterine myonecrosis can be caused by group A βhemolytic streptococcal or clostridial infections (Kaiser, 2018; Soper, 2011).

372
00:49:17,203 --> 00:49:22,941
Those with early-onset disease present with findings listed in Table 50-6.

373
00:49:23,092 --> 00:49:31,517
The mortality rate in these women with gangrene, shown in Figure 50-6 is high, and prompt hysterectomy may be lifesaving.

374
00:49:31,467 --> 00:49:40,442
Group A β-hemolytic streptococci and clostridial colonization or infection also cause toxic-shock syndrome without obvious gangrene .

375
00:49:40,442 --> 00:49:48,454
These are due to either streptococcal toxic shock syndrome–like toxin or clostridial exotoxin that evolved from S aureus .

376
00:49:48,454 --> 00:49:56,879
In many of these cases, bacteremia and widespread tissue invasion develops, but uterine and abdominal incisions are intact.

377
00:49:56,879 --> 00:50:06,892
If uterine necrosis can be excluded—usually by CT scanning—then in our experiences, as well as in others, hysterectomy may not be necessary .

378
00:50:06,892 --> 00:50:14,254
Still, these infections can be highly lethal (Chen, 2019; Yamada, 2010).

379
00:50:14,254 --> 00:50:16,642
Table 50-6.

380
00:50:16,642 --> 00:50:24,567
Clinical Findings in 126 Women with Group A β-Hemolytic Streptococcal Infection Figure 50-6.

381
00:50:24,567 --> 00:50:33,379
A fatal case of group A β-hemolytic Streptococcus pyogenes puerperal infection following an uncomplicated vaginal delivery at term.

382
00:50:33,379 --> 00:50:37,892
The infection caused uterine gangrene and overwhelming sepsis.

383
00:50:37,892 --> 00:50:46,004
Arrows point to overtly “ballooned-out” black gangrenous areas of the postpartum uterus at the time of laparotomy for hysterectomy.

384
00:50:46,004 --> 00:50:55,004
A second exception, necrotizing fasciitis of the episiotomy site or of the abdominal surgical incision, is a surgical emergency.

385
00:50:55,004 --> 00:51:05,054
Described by Gallup and coworkers (2002), these infections are aggressively managed as discussed in Chapter 37 (p. 655).

386
00:51:05,054 --> 00:51:14,354
In one instance, Sinha and colleagues (2015) described a woman with Fournier gangrene who required radical debridement and colostomy.

387
00:51:14,354 --> 00:51:24,342
Third, persistent or aggressive postpartum uterine infection with necrosis, uterine incision dehiscence, and severe peritonitis may lead to sepsis .

388
00:51:24,342 --> 00:51:35,204
In this regard, women following cesarean delivery who are suspected of having peritonitis should be carefully evaluated for uterine incisional necrosis or bowel perforation.

389
00:51:35,204 --> 00:51:42,842
These infections tend to be less aggressive than necrotizing group A streptococcal infections and develop later postpartum.

390
00:51:42,842 --> 00:51:47,704
CT imaging of the abdomen and pelvis can frequently disclose these.

391
00:51:47,704 --> 00:51:52,592
If either is suspected, timely surgical exploration is considered.

392
00:51:52,592 --> 00:52:01,292
With uterine incision necrosis, hysterectomy is usually necessary (Fig. 37-5, p. 657).

393
00:52:01,292 --> 00:52:10,229
Last, peritonitis and sepsis much less commonly may result from a ruptured parametrial, intraabdominal, or ovarian abscess .

394
00:52:10,229 --> 00:52:18,917
Adjunctive Therapy A woman with severe sepsis is supported with continuing crystalloid infusion, blood transfusions, and ventilation.

395
00:52:18,917 --> 00:52:22,179
In some cases, other measures are needed.

396
00:52:22,179 --> 00:52:30,217
Vasoactive drugs are not given unless aggressive fluid treatment fails to correct hypotension and perfusion abnormalities.

397
00:52:30,217 --> 00:52:34,617
First-line vasopressors are norepinephrine and vasopressin .

398
00:52:34,617 --> 00:52:42,767
According to the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2019), norepinephrine has been studied most in pregnant women.

399
00:52:42,767 --> 00:52:53,779
Glucocorticoid use in this setting remains controversial, and some but not all studies show a favorable effect (Marik, 2018; Venkatesh, 2018).

400
00:52:53,779 --> 00:53:05,579
In a recent study by Annane and colleagues (2018), hydrocortisone plus fludrocortisone compared with placebo resulted in a significantly lower mortality rate at 90 days.

401
00:53:05,579 --> 00:53:10,754
Thus, low-dose corticosteroid therapy can be considered with septic shock .

402
00:53:10,754 --> 00:53:17,729
Endotoxin stimulates endothelial cells to upregulate tissue factor and thus procoagulant production .

403
00:53:17,729 --> 00:53:25,367
Consumptive coagulopathy associated with sepsis is discussed in Chapter 44 (p. 776).

404
00:53:25,367 --> 00:53:31,942
At the same time, endotoxin decreases the anticoagulant action of activated protein C.

405
00:53:31,942 --> 00:53:37,854
Several agents developed to block coagulation, however, do not improve outcomes.

406
00:53:37,854 --> 00:53:44,867
Last, induced hypothermia does not improve survival in patients with septic shock and respiratory failure .

407
00:53:44,867 --> 00:53:46,329
Trauma.

408
00:53:46,329 --> 00:53:56,992
Depending on definitions used, 10 to 20 percent of gravidas suffer physical trauma (Al-Thani, 2018; Mendez-Figueroa, 2017).

409
00:53:56,992 --> 00:54:07,917
Moreover, injury-related deaths are the most commonly identified nonobstetrical cause of maternal mortality (Huls, 2018; La Rosa, 2020).

410
00:54:07,917 --> 00:54:17,654
According to Deshpande and coworkers (2017), pregnant victims of trauma are twice as likely to die compared with nonpregnant affected women.

411
00:54:17,654 --> 00:54:19,417
In an audit

412
00:54:19,588 --> 00:54:27,575
from Parkland Hospital, motor vehicle accidents and falls accounted for 85 percent of injuries sustained by gravidas .

413
00:54:27,525 --> 00:54:38,738
From the National Violent Death Reporting System, Palladino and colleagues (2011) found 2 pregnancy-associated suicides per 100,000 live births.

414
00:54:38,738 --> 00:54:44,013
The rate was 3 per 100,000 for pregnancy-associated homicides.

415
00:54:44,013 --> 00:54:48,850
Notably, intimate partner violence may be linked to these suicides .

416
00:54:48,850 --> 00:54:55,388
Last, injury prevention and education of high-risk patients may help decrease morbidity rates .

417
00:54:55,388 --> 00:54:58,300
# Intimate Partner Violence.

418
00:54:58,300 --> 00:55:08,975
According to the CDC, intimate partner violence (IPV) describes physical, sexual, or psychological harm by a current or former partner or spouse .

419
00:55:08,975 --> 00:55:13,100
Such violence affects 1 in 5 women in their lifetime .

420
00:55:13,100 --> 00:55:21,350
One goal in violence prevention for Healthy People 2020 is the reduction of physical abuse directed at women by male partners.

421
00:55:21,350 --> 00:55:25,788
Even more appalling is that IPV continues during pregnancy .

422
00:55:25,788 --> 00:55:32,500
Abuse is linked to poverty, poor education, and use of tobacco, alcohol, and illicit drugs.

423
00:55:32,500 --> 00:55:41,663
Unfortunately, abused women tend to remain with their abusers, and one major risk factor for intimate partner homicide is prior IPV .

424
00:55:41,663 --> 00:55:47,238
Last, women seeking pregnancy termination have a higher incidence of IPV .

425
00:55:47,238 --> 00:55:55,975
In one study, pregnant women hospitalized in California as a result of assault had significantly increased perinatal morbidity rates .

426
00:55:55,975 --> 00:56:02,438
Immediate sequelae were uterine rupture, preterm delivery, and maternal and perinatal death.

427
00:56:02,438 --> 00:56:11,138
Subsequent outcomes included higher rates of placental abruption, preterm and low-birthweight newborns, and other adverse outcomes.

428
00:56:11,138 --> 00:56:18,913
Silverman and associates (2006) reported similar results from the Pregnancy Risk Assessment Monitoring System.

429
00:56:18,913 --> 00:56:25,538
In a metaanalysis, women suffering IPV had threefold increased risk for perinatal death .

430
00:56:25,538 --> 00:56:41,425
The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) recommend universal screening for IPV at the initial prenatal visit, during each trimester, and again at the postpartum visit .

431
00:56:41,425 --> 00:56:48,863
A challenge in these recommendations is that women who refuse to answer the screening question for IPV may be most at risk .

432
00:56:48,863 --> 00:56:55,375
Moreover, the woman who is physically abused tends to present late, if at all, for prenatal care.

433
00:56:55,375 --> 00:56:57,838
# Sexual Assault.

434
00:56:57,838 --> 00:57:09,775
The lifetime prevalence of sexual assault in the United States is 20 percent (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019c; Vrees, 2017).

435
00:57:09,775 --> 00:57:20,575
Satin and associates (1992) reviewed more than 5700 female sexual assault victims in Dallas County and reported that 2 percent were pregnant.

436
00:57:20,575 --> 00:57:23,775
Associated physical trauma is common.

437
00:57:23,775 --> 00:57:29,025
From a forensic standpoint, the evidence collection protocol is not altered .

438
00:57:29,025 --> 00:57:36,000
In addition to attention to physical injuries, exposure to sexually transmitted diseases must be considered.

439
00:57:36,000 --> 00:57:46,950
The CDC (2015, 2016) recommends antimicrobial prophylaxis against gonorrhea, chlamydial infection, and trichomoniasis .

440
00:57:46,950 --> 00:57:53,713
At Parkland Hospital, we also offer at-risk exposed assault victims HIV prophylaxis.

441
00:57:53,713 --> 00:58:10,663
If the woman is not pregnant, another very important aspect is emergency contraception, as recommended by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018a; 2019c) and discussed in Chapter 38 (p. 676).

442
00:58:10,663 --> 00:58:13,050
Table 50-7.

443
00:58:13,050 --> 00:58:25,613
Guidelines for Prophylaxis Against Sexually Transmitted Disease in Pregnant Victims of Sexual Assault Last, the importance of psychological counseling for the rape victim and her family cannot be overemphasized.

444
00:58:25,613 --> 00:58:39,388
As discussed in Chapter 64 (p. 1142), a 30- to 35-percent lifetime risk each for posttraumatic stress disorder, major depression, and suicide contemplation follows sexual assault .

445
00:58:39,388 --> 00:58:42,300
# Automobile Accidents.

446
00:58:42,300 --> 00:58:49,113
At least 3 percent of pregnant women are involved in motor vehicle accidents each year in the United States.

447
00:58:49,113 --> 00:58:54,600
These crashes account for 90 percent of nonviolent blunt trauma in pregnancy .

448
00:58:54,600 --> 00:59:07,800
Thus, they are the most common causes of serious, life-threatening, or fatal blunt trauma during pregnancy (Al-Thani, 2018; Greco, 2019; Petrone, 2017).

449
00:59:07,800 --> 00:59:15,488
Mattox and Goetzl (2005) report these accidents to be the leading cause of traumatic fetal deaths as well.

450
00:59:15,488 --> 00:59:17,963
This also was true from our

451
00:59:18,124 --> 00:59:21,149
experiences from Parkland Hospital .

452
00:59:21,099 --> 00:59:26,299
As with all motor vehicle crashes, alcohol use is often associated.

453
00:59:26,299 --> 00:59:35,974
Up to half of accidents occur without seat-belt use, and many of these deaths would likely be preventable by the three-point restraints shown in Figure 50-7 .

454
00:59:35,974 --> 00:59:41,911
Seat belts prevent contact with the steering wheel, and they reduce abdominal impact pressure.

455
00:59:41,911 --> 00:59:44,324
Figure 50-7.

456
00:59:44,324 --> 00:59:48,974
Illustration showing correct use of three-point automobile restraint.

457
00:59:48,974 --> 00:59:56,249
The upper belt is above the uterus, and the lower belt fits snugly across the upper thighs and well below the uterus.

458
00:59:56,249 --> 01:00:02,049
Original concerns regarding injuries caused by airbag deployment have been somewhat allayed.

459
01:00:02,049 --> 01:00:15,161
In a retrospective cohort study that included 2207 pregnant women in crashes with airbag deployment, perinatal outcomes were not clinically different from 1141 controls without airbags .

460
01:00:15,161 --> 01:00:19,836
Importantly, 96 percent of both groups used seat belts.

461
01:00:19,836 --> 01:00:26,236
Logically, it appears that injuries with airbag deployment are related to the severity of the crash .

462
01:00:26,236 --> 01:00:28,786
# Other Blunt Trauma.

463
01:00:28,786 --> 01:00:33,961
Some other common causes of blunt trauma are falls and aggravated assaults.

464
01:00:33,961 --> 01:00:45,924
In the California review reported by El Kady and associates (2005), approximately a third of pregnant women who were hospitalized for trauma suffered intentionally inflicted injuries.

465
01:00:45,924 --> 01:00:49,061
Less common are blast or crush injury.

466
01:00:49,061 --> 01:00:53,461
With blunt trauma, intraabdominal injuries can be serious.

467
01:00:53,461 --> 01:00:59,561
Even so, bowel injuries are less frequent because of the protective effect of a large uterus.

468
01:00:59,561 --> 01:01:05,649
Still, diaphragmatic, splenic, liver, and kidney damage also may be sustained.

469
01:01:05,649 --> 01:01:16,974
Particularly worrisome is the specter of amnionic-fluid embolism, which has been reported with even mild trauma (Ellingsen, 2007; Pluymakers, 2007).

470
01:01:16,974 --> 01:01:21,924
Retroperitoneal hemorrhage is likely more common than in nonpregnant women .

471
01:01:21,924 --> 01:01:26,249
Orthopedic injuries also are encountered with some regularity .

472
01:01:26,249 --> 01:01:34,374
From the Parkland Hospital trauma unit, 6 percent of 1682 pregnant women evaluated had orthopedic injuries.

473
01:01:34,374 --> 01:01:42,361
Additionally, this subset was at increased risk for placental abruption, preterm delivery, and perinatal mortality.

474
01:01:42,361 --> 01:01:51,874
In a review of 101 pelvic fractures during pregnancy, the maternal mortality rate was 9 percent, and the fetal rate was 35 percent .

475
01:01:51,874 --> 01:02:00,186
In another study of pelvic and acetabular fractures during 15 pregnancies, one maternal and four fetal deaths were reported .

476
01:02:00,186 --> 01:02:04,974
Last, head trauma and neurosurgical care raise unique issues .

477
01:02:04,974 --> 01:02:07,836
# Fetal Injury and Death.

478
01:02:07,836 --> 01:02:12,349
Perinatal death rates rise with the severity of maternal injuries.

479
01:02:12,349 --> 01:02:25,761
Fetal death is more likely with direct fetoplacental injury, maternal shock, pelvic fracture, maternal head injury, or hypoxia (Mendez-Figueroa, 2017; Pearlman, 2008).

480
01:02:25,761 --> 01:02:32,324
In an earlier study, motor vehicle accidents caused 82 percent of fetal deaths from trauma.

481
01:02:32,324 --> 01:02:37,761
Death resulted from placental injury in half and from uterine rupture in 4 percent .

482
01:02:37,761 --> 01:02:45,449
Although uncommon, fetal skull and brain injuries are more likely if the head is engaged and the maternal pelvis is fractured.

483
01:02:45,449 --> 01:02:54,924
Conversely, fetal head injuries, presumably from a contrecoup effect, may be sustained in unengaged vertex or nonvertex presentations.

484
01:02:54,924 --> 01:02:59,611
Fetal skull fractures are rare and best seen using CT imaging .

485
01:02:59,611 --> 01:03:03,199
One example is shown in Figure 50-8.

486
01:03:03,199 --> 01:03:06,724
Intracranial hemorrhage may be another sequela .

487
01:03:06,724 --> 01:03:17,536
Other injuries have included fetal decapitation or incomplete midabdominal fetal transection at midpregnancy (Rowe, 1996; Weir, 2008).

488
01:03:17,536 --> 01:03:19,861
Figure 50-8.

489
01:03:19,861 --> 01:03:25,349
This woman in her third trimester was involved in a high-speed motor vehicle accident.

490
01:03:25,349 --> 01:03:33,286
A. Maximum intensity projection acquired for maternal indications readily identifies fetal skull fractures (arrows).

491
01:03:33,286 --> 01:03:44,211
B. Three-dimensional reformatted computed tomography (CT) image in a bone algorithm demonstrates the fetal skeleton from data acquired during the maternal examination.

492
01:03:44,211 --> 01:03:47,711
Again, the arrow marks one fracture site.

493
01:03:47,711 --> 01:03:51,399
Photograph contributed by Dr. Travis Browning.

494
01:03:51,399 --> 01:03:57,474
Reproduced with permission from Bailey AA, Twickler DM: Perioperative imaging.

495
01:03:57,474 --> 01:04:06,986
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

496
01:04:06,986 --> 01:04:11,474
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

497
01:04:11,474 --> 01:04:13,649
# Placental

498
01:04:13,804 --> 01:04:15,454
Injuries.

499
01:04:15,404 --> 01:04:22,791
Catastrophic events that potentially accompany blunt trauma include placental abruption or placental tear or both.

500
01:04:22,791 --> 01:04:31,416
Placental abruption caused by trauma is likely due to deformation of the elastic myometrium around the relatively inelastic placenta .

501
01:04:31,416 --> 01:04:38,866
This may result from a deceleration injury as the forward-moving uterus meets the immovable steering wheel or seat belt.

502
01:04:38,866 --> 01:04:45,966
Instead, placental injury may follow a contrecoup injury, which is illustrated in Figure 50-9.

503
01:04:45,966 --> 01:04:48,379
Figure 50-9.

504
01:04:48,379 --> 01:04:53,441
Mechanism of placental tear or “fracture” caused by a coup-contrecoup injury.

505
01:04:53,441 --> 01:05:00,204
Although not shown here, forward momentum of the uterus at the time of initial impact propels it forward.

506
01:05:00,204 --> 01:05:04,991
This is followed by a contrecoup, in which forces are directed backward.

507
01:05:04,991 --> 01:05:10,341
In this image, the inelastic placenta fractures against the sacral promontory.

508
01:05:10,341 --> 01:05:19,741
During each phase, tremendous intraabdominal pressure is generated that can lead to abruption, shown here as blood collecting in the retroplacental space.

509
01:05:19,741 --> 01:05:21,354
Inset.

510
01:05:21,354 --> 01:05:26,716
Blood can be forced into placental bed venules and enter maternal circulation.

511
01:05:26,716 --> 01:05:31,829
Such maternofetal hemorrhage may be identified with Kleihauer-Betke testing.

512
01:05:31,829 --> 01:05:39,041
Some degree of abruption complicates 1 to 6 percent of minor injuries and up to 50 percent of major ones .

513
01:05:39,041 --> 01:05:43,866
Abruption is more likely if vehicle speed exceeds 30 mph .

514
01:05:43,866 --> 01:05:50,304
Clinical findings with traumatic abruption may be similar to those for spontaneous placental abruption .

515
01:05:50,304 --> 01:05:59,916
Kettel and coworkers (1988) emphasized that traumatic abruption may be occult and not accompanied by uterine pain, tenderness, or bleeding.

516
01:05:59,916 --> 01:06:08,691
In our experiences with 13 such women at Parkland Hospital, 11 had uterine tenderness, but only five had vaginal bleeding.

517
01:06:08,691 --> 01:06:19,629
Because traumatic abruption is more likely to be concealed and generate higher intrauterine pressures, associated coagulopathy is more often seen than with atraumatic abruption .

518
01:06:19,629 --> 01:06:27,404
Partial separation also may generate uterine activity, which is described more fully later (p. 896).

519
01:06:27,404 --> 01:06:37,866
Evidence of fetal compromise such as fetal tachycardia, sinusoidal pattern, late decelerations, acidosis, and fetal death are other worrisome features.

520
01:06:37,866 --> 01:06:48,816
CT imaging has been shown to be accurate for diagnosis of traumatic abruption (Kopelman, 2013, 2016; Sadro, 2012).

521
01:06:48,816 --> 01:06:58,429
However, because routine placental evaluation by radiologists is not common, these authors did not advocate for CT screening for abruption.

522
01:06:58,429 --> 01:07:06,991
With a placental tear, life-threatening fetal hemorrhage may follow, and blood enters either the amnionic sac or maternal circulation.

523
01:07:06,991 --> 01:07:09,929
As shown in Figure 50-10.

524
01:07:09,929 --> 01:07:12,754
the tear is linear or stellate.

525
01:07:12,754 --> 01:07:19,241
Also shown is a Kleihauer-Betke stain of maternal blood that is used to quantify fetomaternal hemorrhage.

526
01:07:19,241 --> 01:07:29,454
A small amount of fetal-maternal bleeding has been described in up to a third of trauma cases, and in 90 percent of these, the volume is <15 mL .

527
01:07:29,454 --> 01:07:40,604
Parenthetically, atraumatic placental abruption is much less often associated with significant fetomaternal hemorrhage because only minimal fetal blood enters into the intervillous space.

528
01:07:40,604 --> 01:07:45,979
With traumatic abruption, however, massive fetomaternal hemorrhage may follow.

529
01:07:45,979 --> 01:07:54,866
In one study, the risk of associated uterine contractions and preterm labor was 20-fold if there was evidence for a fetomaternal bleed .

530
01:07:54,866 --> 01:08:00,441
With severe fetal bleeding, long-term adverse neurological outcomes are frequent .

531
01:08:00,441 --> 01:08:02,804
Figure 50-10.

532
01:08:02,804 --> 01:08:07,891
A. Partial placental abruption in which the adherent blood clot has been removed.

533
01:08:07,891 --> 01:08:14,879
Note the laceration of the placenta (arrow), which caused fetal death from massive fetomaternal hemorrhage.

534
01:08:14,879 --> 01:08:20,441
B. Kleihauer–Betke stain of a peripheral smear of maternal blood from the same woman.

535
01:08:20,441 --> 01:08:29,866
The dark cells containing hemoglobin that constituted 4.5 percent of red blood cells are fetal in origin, whereas the empty cells are maternal.

536
01:08:29,866 --> 01:08:32,304
# Uterine Rupture.

537
01:08:32,304 --> 01:08:44,316
Blunt trauma leads to uterine rupture in <1 percent of severe cases (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2017a; Greco, 2019).

538
01:08:44,316 --> 01:08:52,041
Rupture is more likely in a previously scarred uterus and is usually associated with a direct impact of substantial force.

539
01:08:52,041 --> 01:08:57,704
Decelerative forces following a 25-mph collision can generate up

540
01:08:57,868 --> 01:09:03,793
to 500 mm Hg of intrauterine pressure in a properly restrained woman .

541
01:09:03,743 --> 01:09:12,718
Clinical findings may be identical to those for placental abruption with an intact uterus, and maternal and fetal deterioration are inevitable.

542
01:09:12,718 --> 01:09:20,555
Weir and colleagues (2008) described supracervical uterine avulsion and fetal transection at 22 weeks.

543
01:09:20,555 --> 01:09:23,230
# Penetrating Trauma.

544
01:09:23,230 --> 01:09:33,393
In a study of 321 pregnant women with abdominal trauma, Petrone (2011) reported a 9-percent incidence of penetrating injuries.

545
01:09:33,393 --> 01:09:39,655
Of these, 77 percent were gunshot wounds, and 23 percent were stab wounds.

546
01:09:39,655 --> 01:09:49,805
The incidence of maternal visceral injury with penetrating trauma is only 15 to 40 percent compared with 80 to 90 percent in nonpregnant individuals .

547
01:09:49,805 --> 01:09:54,880
However, the large, intervening uterus is disproportionately involved .

548
01:09:54,880 --> 01:10:01,655
When the uterus sustains penetrating wounds, the fetus is more likely than the mother to be seriously injured.

549
01:10:01,655 --> 01:10:09,193
The fetus sustains injury in two thirds of cases, whereas maternal visceral damage is seen in only 20 percent.

550
01:10:09,193 --> 01:10:18,405
Still, their seriousness is underscored in that maternal-fetal mortality rates are significantly higher than those seen with blunt abdominal injuries.

551
01:10:18,405 --> 01:10:28,043
Specifically, maternal mortality rates were 7 versus 2 percent, and fetal mortality rates were 73 versus 10 percent, respectively.

552
01:10:28,043 --> 01:10:30,718
# Management of Trauma.

553
01:10:30,718 --> 01:10:36,705
As discussed, maternal and fetal outcomes are directly related to injury severity.

554
01:10:36,705 --> 01:10:47,755
That said, commonly used methods of severity scoring do not account for the significant morbidity and mortality rates related to placental abruption and thus to pregnancy outcomes.

555
01:10:47,755 --> 01:10:57,743
In a study of 582 pregnant women hospitalized for injuries, the injury severity score did not accurately predict adverse pregnancy outcomes .

556
01:10:57,743 --> 01:11:04,430
Importantly, relatively minor injuries were associated with preterm labor and placental abruption.

557
01:11:04,430 --> 01:11:19,068
In a study of 317 women at ≥24 weeks’ gestation who had “minor trauma,” 14 percent had clinically significant uterine contractions requiring extended fetal evaluation past 4 hours .

558
01:11:19,068 --> 01:11:29,618
With few exceptions, treatment priorities in injured pregnant women are multidisciplinary (MacArthur, 2019; Mendez-Figueroa, 2017).

559
01:11:29,618 --> 01:11:34,330
Primary goals are to evaluate and stabilize maternal injuries.

560
01:11:34,330 --> 01:11:41,530
Attention to fetal assessment during the acute evaluation may divert attention from life-threatening maternal injuries .

561
01:11:41,530 --> 01:11:50,255
Basic rules of resuscitation include ventilation, arrest of hemorrhage, and treatment of hypovolemia with crystalloid and blood products.

562
01:11:50,255 --> 01:11:58,943
After midpregnancy, the large uterus is positioned off the great vessels to relieve vessel compression, which decreases cardiac output .

563
01:11:58,943 --> 01:12:12,318
Following emergency resuscitation, fractures, internal injuries, bleeding sites, and placental, uterine, and fetal trauma are sought (Greco, 2019; MacArthur, 2019).

564
01:12:12,318 --> 01:12:17,905
Radiography is not proscribed, but special attention is given each indication.

565
01:12:17,905 --> 01:12:25,805
Not surprisingly, one report observed that pregnant trauma victims had less radiation exposure than nonpregnant controls .

566
01:12:25,805 --> 01:12:32,743
Some advocate screening abdominal sonography followed by CT scanning for positive sonographic findings .

567
01:12:32,743 --> 01:12:38,630
Procedures used include the FAST scan—focused assessment with sonography for trauma.

568
01:12:38,630 --> 01:12:48,080
This is a 5-minute, four- to six-view imaging study that evaluates perihepatic, perisplenic, pelvic, and pericardial views .

569
01:12:48,080 --> 01:13:00,843
In nonpregnant women, if fluid is seen in the upper abdominal views, the hemoperitoneum volume is >500 mL (Branney, 1995; Rodgerson, 2001).

570
01:13:00,843 --> 01:13:05,480
Importantly, this amount has not been corroborated for pregnancy.

571
01:13:05,480 --> 01:13:10,280
In some cases, open peritoneal lavage may be informative .

572
01:13:10,280 --> 01:13:12,730
Figure 50-11.

573
01:13:12,730 --> 01:13:14,593
Fast scan.

574
01:13:14,593 --> 01:13:22,005
Upper quadrant scans show free fluid (blood) between the liver and kidney (Morison pouch) and around the spleen.

575
01:13:22,005 --> 01:13:27,405
The patient had 2500 mL of blood in the peritoneal cavity.

576
01:13:27,405 --> 01:13:33,830
Reproduced with permission from Mendez-Figueroa H, Rouse DJ: Trauma in pregnancy.

577
01:13:33,830 --> 01:13:43,343
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

578
01:13:43,343 --> 01:13:47,830
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

579
01:13:47,830 --> 01:13:53,180
Penetrating injuries in most cases must be evaluated using radiography.

580
01:13:53,180 --> 01:13:57,643
Because clinical response to peritoneal irritation is blunted

581
01:13:57,796 --> 01:14:03,471
during pregnancy, an aggressive approach to exploratory laparotomy is pursued.

582
01:14:03,421 --> 01:14:12,358
Whereas exploration is mandatory for abdominal gunshot wounds, some clinicians advocate close observation for selected stab wounds.

583
01:14:12,358 --> 01:14:16,071
Diagnostic laparoscopy also has been used .

584
01:14:16,071 --> 01:14:21,808
Cesarean Delivery The necessity for cesarean delivery depends on several factors.

585
01:14:21,808 --> 01:14:26,096
Laparotomy itself is not an indication for hysterotomy.

586
01:14:26,096 --> 01:14:37,671
Some considerations include gestational age, fetal condition, extent of uterine injury, and whether the large uterus hinders adequate management of other intraabdominal injuries .

587
01:14:37,671 --> 01:14:48,358
Electronic Monitoring Because fetal well-being may reflect the status of the mother, fetal monitoring is another “vital sign” that helps evaluate the extent of maternal injuries.

588
01:14:48,358 --> 01:14:54,071
Even if the mother is stable, electronic monitoring may suggest placental abruption.

589
01:14:54,071 --> 01:15:05,421
In a study by Pearlman and coworkers (1990), no woman had an abruption if uterine contractions were less often than every 10 minutes within the 4 hours after trauma was sustained.

590
01:15:05,421 --> 01:15:14,308
Almost 20 percent of women who had contractions more frequently than every 10 minutes in the first 4 hours had an associated placental abruption.

591
01:15:14,308 --> 01:15:21,458
In these cases, abnormal tracings were common and included fetal tachycardia and late decelerations.

592
01:15:21,458 --> 01:15:27,333
Conversely, no adverse outcomes were reported in women who had normal monitor tracings .

593
01:15:27,333 --> 01:15:34,958
Importantly, if tocolytics are used for these contractions, they may obfuscate findings, and we do not recommend them.

594
01:15:34,958 --> 01:15:42,746
Because placental abruption usually develops early following trauma, fetal monitoring is begun as soon as the mother is stable.

595
01:15:42,746 --> 01:15:47,483
The ideal duration of posttrauma monitoring is not precisely known.

596
01:15:47,483 --> 01:15:59,808
From data just cited, observation for 4 hours is reasonable if the fetal heart rate tracing is normal and no other sentinel findings such as contractions, uterine tenderness, or bleeding are noted.

597
01:15:59,808 --> 01:16:14,233
Certainly, monitoring should be continued as long as there are uterine contractions, nonreassuring fetal heart patterns, vaginal bleeding, uterine tenderness or irritability, serious maternal injury, or ruptured membranes .

598
01:16:14,233 --> 01:16:19,271
In rare cases, placental abruption has developed days after trauma .

599
01:16:19,271 --> 01:16:22,133
# Fetal-Maternal Hemorrhage.

600
01:16:22,133 --> 01:16:34,621
It is unclear whether routine use of the Kleihauer-Betke or an equivalent test in pregnant trauma victims might modify adverse outcomes associated with fetal anemia, cardiac arrhythmias, and death .

601
01:16:34,621 --> 01:16:45,058
In a retrospective review of 125 pregnant women with blunt injuries, the Kleihauer-Betke test was judged to be of little value during acute trauma management .

602
01:16:45,058 --> 01:16:58,696
Others have reached similar conclusions, although a positive test result with fetal cells composing 0.1 percent predicted uterine contractions or preterm labor (Muench, 2003, 2004).

603
01:16:58,696 --> 01:17:05,008
For the woman who is D-negative, administration of anti-D immunoglobulin should be considered .

604
01:17:05,008 --> 01:17:09,458
This may be omitted if a test result for fetal bleeding is negative.

605
01:17:09,458 --> 01:17:18,708
Even with anti-D immunoglobulin, alloimmunization may still develop if the fetal-maternal hemorrhage exceeds 15 mL of fetal cells.

606
01:17:18,708 --> 01:17:25,283
Immunoglobulin dose calculations are described in Chapter 18 (p. 357).

607
01:17:25,283 --> 01:17:31,608
For the pregnant trauma patient, confirmation of current tetanus immunization status is pertinent.

608
01:17:31,608 --> 01:17:43,471
When indicated, a dose of tetanus toxoid, reduced diphtheria toxoid, and acellular pertussis (Tdap) vaccine is preferred for its neonatal pertussis immunity benefits .

609
01:17:43,471 --> 01:17:45,296
Thermal Injury.

610
01:17:45,296 --> 01:17:50,471
Treatment of the burned gravida is similar to that for nonpregnant individuals .

611
01:17:50,471 --> 01:17:59,121
It is generally agreed that pregnancy does not alter maternal outcome from thermal injury compared with that of nonpregnant women of similar age.

612
01:17:59,121 --> 01:18:05,883
As perhaps expected, maternal and fetal survival parallels the percentage of burned surface area.

613
01:18:05,883 --> 01:18:14,933
Karimi and colleagues (2009) reported higher mortality rates for both with burns from suicide attempts and inhalational injuries.

614
01:18:14,933 --> 01:18:24,283
The composite mortality rate for more than 200 women from six studies rose in a linear fashion as the percentage of burned body surface area increased .

615
01:18:24,283 --> 01:18:33,508
For 20-, 40-, and 60percent burns, the maternal mortality rates were approximately 4, 30, and 93

616
01:18:33,676 --> 01:18:36,113
percent, respectively.

617
01:18:36,063 --> 01:18:42,976
The corresponding fetal mortality rates were 20, 48, and 96 percent, respectively.

618
01:18:42,976 --> 01:18:50,801
With severe burns, the woman usually enters labor spontaneously within a few days to a week and often delivers a stillborn.

619
01:18:50,801 --> 01:18:59,663
Contributory factors are hypovolemia, pulmonary injury, septicemia, and the burn-associated, intense catabolic state .

620
01:18:59,663 --> 01:19:02,051
Figure 50-12.

621
01:19:02,051 --> 01:19:07,551
Maternal and fetal mortality rates by burn severity in 211 women.

622
01:19:07,551 --> 01:19:20,151
Data from Akhtar, 1994; Amy, 1985; Mabrouk, 1977; Maghsoudi, 2006; Rayburn, 1984; Rode, 1990.

623
01:19:20,151 --> 01:19:36,051
Following serious abdominal burns, skin contractures that develop may be painful during a subsequent pregnancy and may even require surgical decompression and split-skin autografts (Mitsukawa, 2015; Radosevich, 2013).

624
01:19:36,051 --> 01:19:40,451
Loss or distortion of nipples may cause problems in breastfeeding.

625
01:19:40,451 --> 01:19:51,451
Mitsukawa and associates (2015) reported that contracture release was indicated with scars spanning >75 percent of the total abdominal area.

626
01:19:51,451 --> 01:20:02,776
Alternatively, normal abdominal tissue expansion due to pregnancy appears to be an excellent source for obtaining skin grafts postpartum to correct scar deformities at other body sites .

627
01:20:02,776 --> 01:20:05,988
# Electrical and Lightning Injuries.

628
01:20:05,988 --> 01:20:11,513
Earlier case reports suggested a high fetal mortality rate with electric shock .

629
01:20:11,513 --> 01:20:23,726
In a prospective cohort study, however, Einarson and associates (1997) showed similar perinatal outcomes in 31 injured women compared with those of noninjured controls.

630
01:20:23,726 --> 01:20:33,301
They concluded that traditional 110-volt North American electrical current likely is less dangerous than the 220-volt currents available in Europe.

631
01:20:33,301 --> 01:20:40,676
Thermal burns with electrocution may be extensive, and a woman with brain death from cardiac arrest has been described .

632
01:20:40,676 --> 01:20:45,563
The pathophysiological effects of lightning injuries can be devastating.

633
01:20:45,563 --> 01:20:55,913
García Gutiérrez and colleagues (2005) reviewed 13 case reports of lightning injuries during pregnancy and cited a 50-percent stillbirth rate.

634
01:20:55,913 --> 01:20:58,851
Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation.

635
01:20:58,851 --> 01:21:11,526
Maternal cardiac arrest complicates from 1 in 12,000 to 36,000 delivery admissions (Balki, 2017; Beckett, 2017; Zelop, 2018).

636
01:21:11,526 --> 01:21:18,938
The most common causes are hemorrhage, anesthesia, heart failure, amnionic-fluid embolism, and sepsis.

637
01:21:18,938 --> 01:21:25,463
Shown in Table 50-8 is the alphabetical A–H checklist of cardiac arrest causes.

638
01:21:25,463 --> 01:21:37,988
General topics regarding planning and equipment have been reviewed by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018b) and the Society for Obstetric Anesthesia and Perinatology .

639
01:21:37,988 --> 01:21:40,288
Table 50-8.

640
01:21:40,288 --> 01:21:55,263
Possible Etiologies for Cardiac Arrest During Pregnancy Special considerations for cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) conducted in the second half of pregnancy are outlined in the American Heart Association 2010 guidelines .

641
01:21:55,263 --> 01:22:26,388
The committee acknowledges the following as standards: (1) relieve possible vena caval compression by left lateral uterine displacement; (2) administer 100-percent oxygen; (3) establish intravenous access above the diaphragm; (4) assess for hypotension that warrants therapy, which is defined as systolic blood pressure <100 mm Hg or <80 percent of baseline; and (5) review possible causes of critical illness and treat these as early as possible.

642
01:22:26,388 --> 01:22:32,276
The position of the heart for external compressions is not different from that in nonpregnant women .

643
01:22:32,276 --> 01:22:40,126
In nonpregnant women, external chest compressions result in a cardiac output that is approximately 30 percent of normal.

644
01:22:40,126 --> 01:22:46,076
However, in late pregnancy, this may be less because of uterine aortocaval compression .

645
01:22:46,076 --> 01:22:51,026
Thus, uterine displacement should accompany other resuscitative efforts.

646
01:22:51,026 --> 01:23:00,388
This can be accomplished by tilting the operating table laterally, by placing a wedge under the right hip, or by pushing the uterus to the left manually .

647
01:23:00,388 --> 01:23:07,213
Components of highquality cardiopulmonary resuscitation in pregnancy are shown in Table 50-9 .

648
01:23:07,213 --> 01:23:09,576
Table 50-9.

649
01:23:09,576 --> 01:23:16,101
High-Quality Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation in Pregnancy # Perimortem Cesarean Delivery.

650
01:23:16,101 --> 01:23:28,351
The maternal survival rate from cardiac arrest is 60 to 70 percent, and many deaths are associated with out-of-hospital arrest (Beckett, 2017; He, 2021).

651
01:23:28,351 --> 01:23:32,713
Because of pregnancy-induced hindrances on CPR efforts,

652
01:23:32,884 --> 01:23:40,621
emergent perimortem cesarean delivery for improved maternal resuscitation and fetal salvage is recommended by most.

653
01:23:40,571 --> 01:23:54,784
Specifically, the American Heart Association and the Society for Obstetric Anesthesia and Perinatology recommend operative delivery within 4 to 5 minutes of beginning CPR if the fetus is viable and circulation is not restored .

654
01:23:54,784 --> 01:24:08,384
In women delivered by perimortem cesarean, neurologically intact neonatal survival and the cardiac arrest-to-delivery interval are inversely related (Beckett, 2017; Katz, 2012).

655
01:24:08,384 --> 01:24:28,484
Specifically, of newborns delivered within 5 minutes of arrest, 98 percent are neurologically intact; within 6 to 15 minutes, 83 percent are intact; within 16 to 25 minutes, 33 percent are intact; and within 26 to 35 minutes, only 25 percent are intact .

656
01:24:28,484 --> 01:24:33,346
This serious and sometimes contentious issue is far from evidence based.

657
01:24:33,346 --> 01:24:44,821
For example, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) recommends consideration for perimortem cesarean delivery beginning at 4 minutes.

658
01:24:44,821 --> 01:24:52,659
Katz and associates (2005) reviewed 38 perimortem cesarean deliveries with a “large selection bias.

659
01:24:52,659 --> 01:25:04,259
They concluded that these reports supported—but “fell far from proving”—that perimortem cesarean delivery within 4 minutes of maternal cardiac arrest improves maternal and fetal outcomes.

660
01:25:04,259 --> 01:25:16,734
Even so, as emphasized by Clark (1997) and Rose (2015) and their coworkers, and in our experiences, these goals rarely can be met in actual practice.

661
01:25:16,734 --> 01:25:27,621
For example, many cases of cardiac arrest occur in uncontrolled circumstances, and thus, the time to CPR initiation alone would consume the first 5 minutes.

662
01:25:27,621 --> 01:25:39,934
Thus “crash” cesarean delivery would supersede resuscitative efforts, would possibly be done without appropriate anesthesia or surgical equipment, and might lead to otherwise preventable maternal death.

663
01:25:39,934 --> 01:25:50,546
Moreover, the distinction between a perimortem versus postmortem cesarean operation is imperative (Katz, 2012; Rose, 2015).

664
01:25:50,546 --> 01:26:00,746
Last, in the balance, any choice may favor survival of the mother over the fetus, or vice versa, and thus there are immediate unresolvable ethical concerns.

665
01:26:00,746 --> 01:26:09,846
Katz (2012) and Zelop and colleagues (2018) have provided scholarly reviews of perimortem cesarean delivery.

666
01:26:09,846 --> 01:26:13,784
Successful CPR can have morbid complications.

667
01:26:13,784 --> 01:26:17,434
Severe neurological injury is the most feared .

668
01:26:17,434 --> 01:26:24,371
Cox and coworkers (2018) described liver lacerations in 3 of 7 survivors.

669
01:26:24,371 --> 01:26:28,384
Takotsubo cardiomyopathy also has been reported .

670
01:26:28,384 --> 01:26:31,034
# Maternal Brain Death.

671
01:26:31,034 --> 01:26:40,109
Occasionally, a pregnant woman with a supposedly healthy intact fetus will be kept on somatic support to await fetal viability or maturity.

672
01:26:40,109 --> 01:26:44,809
This is discussed in Chapter 63 (p. 1138).

673
01:26:44,809 --> 01:26:46,709
Envenomation.

674
01:26:46,709 --> 01:26:59,934
According to their review, Brown and coworkers (2013) reported that clinically significant envenomations in pregnant women are from snakes, spiders, scorpions, jellyfish, and hymenoptera.

675
01:26:59,934 --> 01:27:04,971
This last group includes bees, wasps, hornets, and ants.

676
01:27:04,971 --> 01:27:08,646
Adverse outcomes are related to maternal effects.

677
01:27:08,646 --> 01:27:19,471
Available evidence supports a venom-specific approach that incorporates symptomatic care, antivenom administration when appropriate, anaphylaxis treatment, and fetal assessment .

678
01:27:19,471 --> 01:27:26,459
One management scheme for North American snakebites was provided by Lei and associates (2015).

